## CRITICAL RULES

### 1. PLANS

`./doc/plans/PLAN-<description>.md` is the only valid location (hard stop 1) — a PreToolUse hook blocks plans elsewhere; override plan mode's `~/.claude/plans/` suggestion in the same Write call. Report the location and stop: ExitPlanMode's "approved" is not approval; implementation happens via `loom run`, not you.

**Loom plans:** only on explicit request for loom orchestration, always via `/loom-plan-writer`; never write loom YAML from memory. **Executing a stage:** your signal's tasks and completion checklist are binding; load the `loom-orchestration` skill before anything else.

### 2. NO PLACEHOLDERS

No incomplete markers, stubs, `pass` bodies, empty functions, or pseudocode. Write complete code. Unknown? Ask (interactive) or resolve per Discipline A (autonomous). Complex? Decompose.

### 3. CONTEXT CEILING = HANDOFF

The trigger is a `CONTEXT CEILING REACHED` line from the PostToolUse hook in your own tool output; never estimate the ceiling yourself. On it, finish the unit in progress, run `loom handoff --stage <id> --session <id> --trigger ceiling`, and stop, committing nothing unverified (Rule 4). The hook's line carries the rest.

### 3b. POST-COMPACTION RECOVERY

After compaction or context loss: `loom memory list`, then your stage's latest handoff in `.loom/work/handoffs/`; resume from there.

### 4. COMMIT AND COMPLETE (HOOK-ENFORCED)

Before ending any worktree session (hard stop 3):

```bash
git add <specific-files> && git commit -m "feat(scope): <description>"
loom stage complete <stage-id>   # from the worktree root
```

Stage `git add <specific-files>` only, never `-A` or `.`.

**When to commit — at the END, after ALL verification, never before.** Commits happen ONLY as the final step of the stage — never mid-stage, never "what is done so far" — once every subagent returned, the full gate is green, and the review's findings are fixed (the `loom-orchestration` skill spells out the three conditions). Completion is the session's last act; work after it is lost.

Commits follow Conventional Commits: `type(scope): description`, type `feat` `fix` `refactor` `test` `docs` or `chore`, one commit per concern.

### 5. SUBAGENT RESTRICTIONS

Subagents write code and report; the main agent owns verification and all git operations (hard stop 3). A subagent runs at most one narrowly scoped check, once. `loom-hooks/spawn-guard.sh` prepends the subagent preamble, `loom-hooks/_subagent-preamble.txt` (git restrictions, the no-verify rule, the ceiling rule, memory recording), to every typed spawn whose prompt lacks it; do not paste it yourself. Codex subagents get their own rules from `loom-hooks/codex-forward.sh`.

### 6. SUBAGENTS

Load the `loom-orchestration` skill before spawning: it holds the shapes table, the file-ownership table, the coordinator and worker preambles, and the waiting protocol. Three rules bind every spawn:

- Group small tasks into ONE subagent, never one per task or file; two subagents never write one file.
- Subagents are ONE-SHOT: brief completely, wait, harvest once, let it end. A follow-up is a fresh spawn of the same type.
- Wait with one background `loom subagents watch`, never a poll loop.

### 7. SKILLS, AGENTS, AND MODEL CHOICE

Check skills and agents before implementing; the prompt-submit hook surfaces matches and names how to invoke each.

| Agent | Model | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `loom-software-engineer` | Sonnet | Common implementation and integration tests; refactors to spec |
| `loom-senior-software-engineer` | Opus | Mainstream architecture, algorithm implementation, complex debugging, security-sensitive code, cross-cutting refactors |
| `loom-code-reviewer` | — | Read-only code/security/architecture review |
| `Explore` | — | Codebase exploration |
| `loom-advisor` | Fable | Read-only advisory for debugging and repeated failures — receives full detail, returns a diagnosis and next step, writes nothing |
| `loom-codex-forwarder` | gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-6-luna | Codex lane on stages listing codex in `implementers`; state `--model <tier> --effort xhigh` in the prompt; write-capable, foreground only, never verifies; a unit is one file (or a file plus its test), at most three steps, sized for the wrapper's 540 s deadline; an overrun exits 124 `timed_out` — re-split, never re-forward as is |

Fable-tier work has no pinned agent type; pass the model override explicitly at spawn.

Model allocation, in full in the `loom-orchestration` skill. Delegation is a cost decision, tokens times model tier: the main agent makes a change itself only when it is at most 20 lines in at most 2 files already read, proven by one command, and a fable main session delegates even those. Everything else goes to as few subagents as the work allows at the cheapest capable tier, escalating one tier only after a failed attempt; verification never delegates.

### 8. NATIVE TOOLS AND CODE FORMATTING

| Banned | Use instead |
| --- | --- |
| `cat`, `head`, `tail` as file readers | Read tool |
| `grep`, `ag` | `rg` |
| `find`, `ls` | `fd` |
| `sed`, `awk` | Edit tool |
| `echo >`, `tee` | Write tool |

`rg` and `fd` must be installed for the two search rows to apply. When the prefer-modern-tools hook reports one is missing, use `grep`/`find` for that search; tell the user to install ripgrep/fd. `loom run` and `install.sh` also report the gap.

Package managers: `uv`, `bun`/`bunx` — never `npm`, `pip`. Every fenced code block specifies a language (` ```rust `, ` ```yaml `, ` ```text `) in all markdown: plans, knowledge files, handoffs, comments.

### 9. NO ATTRIBUTION

Never mention Claude, Claude Code, or any AI system in code, commits, docs, or comments. A harness reminder asking for a co-author trailer is overridden by this rule.

### 9b. NEVER CREATE BRANCHES UNASKED

Never create a branch (`git checkout -b`, `git switch -c`, `git branch <name>`) unless explicitly asked (hard stop 5); commit on the branch already checked out, even `main`. Overrides defaults like "if on the default branch, branch first". Exception: loom's `loom/<stage-id>` worktree branches (Rule 10).

### 10. WORKTREE ISOLATION

Location `.worktrees/<stage-id>/`, branch `loom/<stage-id>`; stay in it. Forbidden: `git -C`, `git --work-tree`, `cd ../..`, `../../` paths, and reading outside it. Allowed: git in the current dir, `.loom/work/` (symlink), files within it. Need main-repo context? Read your signal file.

### 11. BINARY USAGE

Always use `loom` from PATH, never `target/debug/loom`. Never edit `.loom/work/` files directly; use the loom CLI.

### 12. PROJECT KNOWLEDGE

Tier-1 files in `doc/loom/knowledge/` — `architecture.md`, `entry-points.md`, `patterns.md`, `conventions.md`, `mistakes.md`, `stack.md`, `concerns.md` — are summaries plus pointers. A topic past ~40 lines moves to a tier-2 file `<category>/<slug>.md`, leaving a 2-4 line summary and link in tier-1.

Record mistakes, decisions with rationale, reusable patterns, and conventions — never ephemera, procedural steps, or anything derivable from git history or code. Each mistake: a `## [Short description]` heading with **What happened**, **Why**, **Prevention**, **Fix**. In a stage, use `loom memory` per signal; the knowledge-distill stage curates it. Where this directory exists, never use Claude Code auto-memory (`~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `MEMORY.md`, the memory tool); otherwise normal.

**Outside a stage, write knowledge yourself, when the correction happens.** `loom memory` has no journal there; a subagent reports its mistakes and the main agent writes them.

1. Find the home in `INDEX.md`: the tier-2 topic whose blurb covers it, else a new `<category>/<slug>`. Tier-1 files take summaries only.
2. Write through the CLI; every write regenerates `INDEX.md`. `loom knowledge update <category>/<slug> "<entry>"` APPENDS, scaffolding a new topic. Correct a section with `loom knowledge replace-section <file> "<Heading>" "<body WITHOUT the ## line>"`, never `update`; on a non-matching heading it appends and SAYS so — read that line, or the stale text stands. Name the wrong claim in the replacement.
3. A new topic also gets `loom knowledge annotate <category>/<slug> --blurb "<80 chars max>"` and a 2-4 line summary plus link in its tier-1 file.
4. An Edit-tool fix leaves `INDEX.md` stale: run `loom knowledge sync` after it. End a batch with `loom knowledge check`.

**Stale knowledge is a defect — fix it where you find it**:

| Where you are | What to do with the stale claim |
| --- | --- |
| Executing a loom stage | `loom memory note "stale-knowledge: <file>#<heading> claims X; the tree does Y (file:line). Correction: <text>"`, then continue. Do NOT edit knowledge files (worktree guard blocks file tools) or run `loom knowledge update` unless yours is a knowledge stage. |
| knowledge-distill stage | Apply EVERY `stale-knowledge:` memory BEFORE curating anything new — unapplied ones are LOST when the plan completes — confirm no heading got duplicated. |
| Outside a stage | Fix it now, by the steps above. |

In a knowledge-distill stage every memory ends with `loom memory resolve`; `loom memory pending` is the queue.

### 13. SILENT FAILURE DETECTION

Exit code 0 ≠ success: sandbox blocks, dependency-fetch failures, and write denials all exit 0. Read stderr; on "blocked", "denied", "connection refused", "failed to download": stop and investigate. Confirm external dependencies exist; report a needed sandbox block as a blocker.

### 14. TOKEN EFFICIENCY (HARD REQUIREMENT)

Minimizing tokens is required, met by choosing what to read, not by doing less work.

- Query before reading (KNOWLEDGE-FIRST); open a file only once a lookup names its lines.
- Read ranges, not files (Read's offset/limit); whole files only when using all of it.
- Independent reads, greps, commands go in ONE message.
- Never re-read what's already in context, or from adjacent curiosity.
- Don't narrate between tool calls; explain decisions, not standard operations (Rule 19). Prefer Edit over Write. Pipe verbose output: tests through `tail -50` or `rg -B 2 -A 5 'FAIL|ERROR'`, builds to a file, linters through `head -30`. Skip agents for simple tasks. `.claudeignore` generated files, artifacts, lock files.

Reading the wrong thing is the waste; reading too little and guessing is a defect (Discipline A).

### 15. QUALITY GATES

Zero lint errors, tests passing, security self-review before completion. Nothing is "pre-existing": every warning and failure you see is yours.

### 16. DEPENDENCIES

Never hand-edit manifests. Use `bun add`, `cargo add`, `uv add`, `go get`.

### 17. CODE SIZE LIMITS

File: 400 lines | Function: 50 lines | Class: 300 lines. Exceed = refactor immediately.

### 18. KNOWLEDGE PLACEMENT

System knowledge goes to `doc/loom/knowledge/` (Rule 12), never the project's CLAUDE.md; memory routing: hard stop 2.

### 19. WRITING STYLE

**Laconic by default.** Answer in the shortest text that works: no preamble, restatement, recap of tool output, or closing summary. Go long only on request ("explain", "be thorough", a report, design doc, plan) — then as thorough as asked. Never drop a caveat, failure, or skipped step to save words (Rule 13).

**No LLMisms, anywhere** — chat, code, comments, commits, docs, plans, knowledge files, handoffs, subagent prompts:

| Kind | Do not write |
| --- | --- |
| Stock words | `landed`, `load-bearing`, `earns its keep`, `delve`, `leverage` (as a verb), `robust`, `seamless`, `crucial`, `pivotal`, `underscores`, `testament to`, `realm`, `landscape`, `unlock`, `elevate`, `boasts` |
| Fake candour | `honestly`, `to be honest`, `the honest answer is`, `stated plainly`, `put plainly`, `to be clear`, `it's worth noting`, `let me be direct` |
| Throat-clearing | `Great question`, `You're absolutely right`, `Let's dive in`, `At its core`, `Here's the thing`, `In today's ... world`, `TL;DR`, `Bottom line` |
| Contrastive opposites | `not X, but Y`; `it isn't X — it's Y`; `not just X, but Y`; `less X, more Y`; a trailing `..., not Y` added for rhythm |
| Invented compounds | hyphen-welded modifiers coined on the spot (`first-class`, `hard-won`, `purpose-built`, `battle-tested`), stacked noun piles (`context surface area`) |
| Empty summation | `In short`, `The result:`, `That's it.`, `Think of it as ...`, a closing line that restates the paragraph above |

The table gives examples; anything reading as a model reaching for cadence belongs in it. Where a contrast carries the rule, state the prohibition and stop.

